{
  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "items": [
        "people: One person, including eligible prospects who never purchase.",
        "subscription_periods: One scheduled monthly cycle; a paid outcome establishes retained membership for that cycle.",
        "transactions: One payment or refund event; each payment is one order and refunds reuse its order ID.",
        "product_events: One observed product/support interaction; no event does not mean no subscription."
      ],
      "title": "Every key has a grain"
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "items": [
        "campaign_spend has a composite primary key: campaign_id + spend_date. Do not join its daily rows directly to every acquired customer.",
        "campaign_metadata groups spending by campaign_id and retains its channel and target_need. The source contract verifies that one campaign has one channel and target need.",
        "A refund's related_transaction_id refers to transactions.transaction_id within the same financial ledger. A payment leaves that optional reference blank."
      ],
      "title": "Campaign IDs are not unique in raw spending"
    },
    {
      "dot": "orange",
      "items": [
        "Cardinality describes how many child records can reference one parent. A known person can have no purchase, event or feedback.",
        "acquisition_attribution has at most one row per person; its optional credited_campaign_id is blank for organic credit.",
        "experiment_assignments has a composite key: experiment_id + person_id. Its tested_campaign_id references campaign_metadata.campaign_id, not campaign_spend. Its people include nonbuyers, who must remain in the analysis.",
        "Shared IDs make documented joins possible. They do not turn recorded credit into causal evidence or a complete journey."
      ],
      "title": "Read the links without changing the denominator"
    },
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "items": [
        "transactions.related_transaction_id \u2192 transactions.transaction_id. This optional self-reference links a refund to its original payment. The FK is retained in the table; the native ERD renderer does not draw self-entity edges."
      ],
      "title": "Refunds reference the same ledger"
    }
  ],
